From: Roy Klopper <roy.klopper@stealthscale.io>
Subject: [PATCH] bash-engine: count a command that spans lines as one line

The parser marks every line of a quoted word that spans lines as code,
apart from the "..." of an assignment, where it marks the closing line
and not the first. Bash reports one line for such a command: the first
line since 5.3, and before that the line on which the command's second
word ends. The lines bash does not report stay at zero hits, and the
line it does report is often one the parser did not mark. A multi-line
awk program, a printf with a string that spans lines or a value with a
newline in it each cost a few lines of coverage that no test can earn.

The parser also marks "fi ;;", "esac ;;", "done < <(cmd)", "done | cmd"
and "} 2>/dev/null" as code. Bash reports no line for these.

- Track the quote a line ends in, for "...", '...' and $'...', with
  their escapes. The lines after the first are no code, and a hit on
  any of them is credited to the first line. The same for the lines of
  a backslash continuation.
- Treat a closing keyword followed by the terminator of a case item, a
  redirection, a pipe or & as no code. "} || return 1" and "fi; cmd"
  stay code.
- Take body+="${name}() {" for the string it starts, not for a function
  definition, so that the word it opens is tracked.
- Treat a [[ ]] test that spans lines like arithmetic: bash reports the
  line it ends on, so only that line is code. Do the same for (( ))
  arithmetic without a $ in front.
- Move the expectation of the tests from the closing line of a
  multi-line word to its first line.
